-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ## Attaching a dataset
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC In order to create a dashboard, a dataset must be attached, which we'll do in this section. For this example, we'll use the *dimcustomer* table.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC 1. From the left sidebar, select **New &gt; Dashboard** (open in a new tab so you can continue to follow along here). This brings you to the canvas view for laying out your dashboard components.
-- MAGIC 1. Before adding and configuring components, let's associate a dataset by selecting the **Data** tab.<br>
-- MAGIC    ![Data tab](Includes/images/dashboard/datatab.png)
-- MAGIC 1. Let's use a table as the dataset by clicking **Add data source**.<br>
-- MAGIC    ![Data tab](Includes/images/dashboard/adddatasource.png)
-- MAGIC 1. In the **Add Data source** dialog, locate and select your *dimcustomer* table, and click **Confirm**.<br>
-- MAGIC    ![Data tab](Includes/images/dashboard/selectdimcustomertable.png)
-- MAGIC 1. Back in the **Data** tab, we see the newly added *dimcustomer* dataset. With a SQL warehouse selected, run the default query to view the contents of the selected table.<br>
-- MAGIC    ![Data tab](Includes/images/dashboard/runquery.png)
-- MAGIC

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ## Adding visualizations
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Once a dashboard has data to visualize, we can begin the fun work of populating it with components that bring the dashboard to life.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC 1. Back in the canvas, locate and click the **Add a visualization** button near the bottom.<br>
-- MAGIC    ![Add a visualization](Includes/images/dashboard/addvisualization.png)
-- MAGIC 1. Select a position for your visualization. Use plain language to describe what you'd like to see. For example: *Show me the distribution of customers based on gender.*<br>
-- MAGIC    ![Add a visualization](Includes/images/dashboard/withprompt.png)
-- MAGIC 1. Submit your prompt and a visualization will be generated in response. At this point, you can accept the visualization as-is, reject it, or perform some advanced configuration using the controls on the right sidebar.<br>
-- MAGIC    ![Add a visualization](Includes/images/dashboard/piechart.png)
-- MAGIC    * For example, let's alter the transform to group by country rather than gender.<br>
-- MAGIC      ![Adjust transform](Includes/images/dashboard/adjusttransform.png)
-- MAGIC    * As a result, we get a correspondingly adjusted visualization.<br>
-- MAGIC      ![Group by country](Includes/images/dashboard/adjustedtransform.png)
-- MAGIC    * Before proceeding, revert the visualization to group by gender, and click **Accept**.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ## Filtering
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC AI/BI dashboards support the ability to filter data in the dataset, using the following filter types:
-- MAGIC * Multiple values
-- MAGIC * Single value
-- MAGIC * Date picker
-- MAGIC * Date range picker
-- MAGIC * Text entry
-- MAGIC * Range slider
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC You can connect the filters to one or more datasets. The filter will then be applied to all visualizations built on the selected dataset. Filter selection is also cascaded across all other filters. 
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC 1. Locate and click the **Add a filter** button near the bottom.<br>
-- MAGIC    ![Add a visualization](Includes/images/dashboard/addfilter.png)
-- MAGIC 1. Select a position for your filter, which will populate the canvas with an area to configure the filter.<br>
-- MAGIC    ![Add a visualization](Includes/images/dashboard/positionfilter.png)
-- MAGIC 1. In the right sidebar, configure the filter:
-- MAGIC    * Set **Filter** to *Multiple values*.
-- MAGIC    * Add a field, and select *city*.<br>
-- MAGIC    ![Configure filter](Includes/images/dashboard/configurefilter.png)
-- MAGIC 1. Your filter is now usable. For example, if you select *New York*, the visualization will update based on filtering the dataset. In other words, you'll see the gender distribution for the selected city (or cities).<br>
-- MAGIC    ![Configure filter](Includes/images/dashboard/newyorkgenders.png)

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ## Collaborating
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC All new AI/BI dashboards begin as a draft. This means that any work you do will not impact published dashboards (if any). You can also share these drafts with others in your workspace and collaborate with each other. 
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC 1. Click the **Share** button in the upper right corner.<br>
-- MAGIC    ![Share button](Includes/images/dashboard/sharebutton.png)
-- MAGIC 1. Use the dialog to configure which users or groups you wish to collaborate with. You can choose the permissions to grant to each entity.<br>
-- MAGIC    ![Add a visualization](Includes/images/dashboard/configuresharing.png)

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ## Publishing
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC AI/BI dashboards can be published to make a clean copy of the dashboard available to users within and outside of your Databricks workspace. 
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC 1. Click the **Publish** button in the upper right corner.<br>
-- MAGIC    ![Share button](Includes/images/dashboard/publishbutton.png)
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC 1. The **Publish** dialog appears. The biggest choice to make here is how to deal with the credentials. You have two options:
-- MAGIC    * **Share data permission (default):** all viewers of the dashboard run queries using your credentials and compute. This will allow users to see the dashboard even if they don’t have access to the data or SQL warehouse. However, this may expose data to users that haven’t been explicitly granted access to those resources. **This option must be selected if sharing with users outside of your Databricks workspace.**
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC    * **Individual data permission:** all viewers of the dashboard run queries using their own credentials. Viewers will need access to the attached SQL warehouse, and the associated data to view the dashboard. These requirements imply that this option can only be used for users within your Databricks workspace.<br>
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC    ![Add a visualization](Includes/images/dashboard/newpublishdialog.png)
-- MAGIC 1. With **Share data permission** selected, click **Publish**.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC 1. You'll be provided with a summary of the shared dashboard, including an option to copy the link that you can share with others.<br>
-- MAGIC    ![Add a visualization](Includes/images/dashboard/shareddialog.png)

